<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Mongolia</h1><p class="st-meta">1992 CE – present · capital Ulaanbaatar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The democratic Mongolian state created by the 1992 constitution after the peaceful end of communist rule.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Peaceful demonstrations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulaanbaatar">Ulaanbaatar</a> in the winter of 1989 to 1990 forced the ruling party of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mongolian-peoples-republic">Mongolian People's Republic</a> to give up its monopoly of power. Multi-party elections followed in 1990, and a new constitution adopted in 1992 renamed the country simply Mongolia, with a parliament, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-great-khural">State Great Khural</a>, and a directly elected president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The loss of Soviet aid caused severe hardship in the early 1990s as Mongolia moved to a market economy and privatised its herds. Mining, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyu-tolgoi">Oyu Tolgoi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, later drove rapid growth and made the economy dependent on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> revived, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> became a national symbol, and a 'third neighbour' foreign policy sought partners beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and China.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Power has changed hands repeatedly through elections, though politics has been marked by corruption scandals and protests, notably in 2008. Mongolia remains a parliamentary democracy with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulaanbaatar">Ulaanbaatar</a>, where nearly half the population now lives.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mongolia.jpg" alt="Mongolia" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mongolia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1992 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ulaanbaatar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Punsalmaagiin Ochirbat, Natsagiin Bagabandi, Tsakhiagiin Elbegdorj</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism, Mongolian shamanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mongolian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>State of Mongolia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2009&amp;focus=mongolia" aria-label="Mongolia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7170 500 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mongolia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7490 756L7490 756"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7490 756h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7490 756h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7490 756h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ulaanbaatar. <a href="/atlas?year=2009&amp;focus=mongolia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
